[{"title":"Falcons","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/#Falcons","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Falcons","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"This is the documentation of Falcons(Fast Algorithm for Locus Computing ON the Sky), a package for fast simulation of satellite observations."},{"title":"Falcons","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"CMB polarimetric satellites such as LiteBIRD set up an appropriate scan strategy to reduce systematic errors, and Falcons can calculate all-sky hit maps, crosslink maps, etc. by simply setting the parameters of this scan strategy. It also supports observations with multi-channel detectors."},{"title":"Installation","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/#Installation","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Falcons","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"From the Julia REPL, run"},{"title":"Falcons","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"import Pkg\nPkg.add(\"Falcons\")"},{"title":"Tutorial","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/#Tutorial","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Falcons","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"The tutorial is available on the github page in jupyternotebook format. Please refer to here."},{"title":"Documentation","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/#Documentation","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Falcons","page":"Falcons","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/index-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"The documentation was built using Documenter.jl."},{"title":"Mapmake","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/#Mapmake","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/#Pointings","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"To obtain TOD from Sky map, it is recommended to create a Pointing structure. The elemets are defined as:"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"mutable struct Pointings\n    x::AbstractFloat\n    y::AbstractFloat\n    z::AbstractFloat\n    θ::AbstractFloat # position of sky\n    φ::AbstractFloat # position of sky\n    Ω::Int           # Sky pixel index\n    ψ::AbstractFloat # Crossing angle\n    ϕ::AbstractFloat # HWP angle\n    ξ::AbstractFloat # mod2pi(2ϕ) + ψ\nend"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"It is convenient to use the pointings for the actual generation."},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"pointings(resol::Resolution, θ, φ, ψ, ϕ)"},{"title":"Available bolometric equations","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/#Available-bolometric-equations","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"true_signal(p::Pointings, maps::PolarizedHealpixMap, pixbuf, weightbuf)\ntrue_signal(p::Pointings, maps::PolarizedHealpixMap)\ninterp_signal(p::Pointings, maps::PolarizedHealpixMap)\ninterp_signal(p::Pointings, maps::PolarizedHealpixMap, pixbuf, weightbuf)"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"true_signal returns TOD which is defined by d_j = I(Omega) + Q(Omega)cos(2xi_j) + U(Omega)sin(2xi_j), where Omega shows sky pixel and xiequiv2phi+psi shows effective crossing angle which takes in to account the satellite crossing angle(psi) and its HWP angle(phi)."},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"interp_signal returns interpolated TOD which is defined pointings i.e. theta and varphi."},{"title":"Map-maker","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/#Map-maker","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"The binned_mapmake is available. "},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"binned_mapmake(ss::ScanningStrategy, division::Int, inputinfo::Falcons.InputInfo, signal)"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"The signal argument requires a function which has Pointings and PolarizedHealpixMap as arguments in the order. For example,"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Mapmake-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"binned_mapmake(ss::ScanningStrategy, division::Int, inputinfo::Falcons.InputInfo, true_signal)"},{"title":"Scanning strategy","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/#Scanning-strategy","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"To define the scanning strategy for a satellite, set the ScanningStrategy structure."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"mutable struct ScanningStrategy{T<:AbstractFloat, I<:Int, AS<:AbstractString}\n    nside::I\n    duration::I\n    sampling_rate::T\n    alpha::T\n    beta::T\n    gamma::T\n    prec_rpm::T\n    spin_rpm::T\n    hwp_rpm::T\n    start_point::AS\n    start_angle::T\n    coord::AS\n    quat::Vector{Vector{Float64}}\n    name::Vector{String}\n    info::DataFrame\nend"},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"You can generate ScanningStrategy structure using gen_ScanningStrategy() function."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"This initial value of component of ScanningStrategy can be changed by specifying the gen_ScanningStrategy() argument when declaring ss like below."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"ss = gen_ScanningStrategy(alpha=60, prec_rpm=0.001, sampling_rate=5.0)"},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"In addition, you can directly access ss and interactively change its value."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"ss.nside = 256 #You can change a value\nss.spin_rpm = 0.04"},{"title":"Generate pointings","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/#Generate-pointings","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"The information about the orientation of a satellite at a certain time is called pointing. The pointing is defined by (theta phi psi), where theta and phi are parameters of the 3D polar coordinates and psi is the angle between the scan direction and the meridian of the sky."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"Once the scanning strategy is determined, computing the pointing is straightforward."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"pointings = get_pointings(ss::ScanningStrategy, start::Int, stop::Int)\npix_tod, psi_tod, time_array = get_pointing_pixels(ss::ScanningStrategy, start::Int, stop::Int)"},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"Enter an integer value for the time to be calculated in the start and stop fields. The get_pointings() returns (theta phi psi) that indicate their pointing in chronological order. These are stored as an array of dictionary type, and can be accessed as follows."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"In[]: pointings[\"theta\"]\nOut[]: 14400×1 Matrix{Float64}:\n 1.1344640137963142\n 1.134461356131033\n 1.1344533831585348\n 1.1344400949488544\n..."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"The pointings[\"psi\"] contains the scanning angle according to the COSMO(HEALPix) definition. And pointings[\"time\"] contains the time used in the calculation."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"The get_pointing_pixels() does not allocate theta and phi arrays internally, but only allocates the minimum number of arrays needed to allocate the pixel TOD. Therefore, it runs faster than get_pointings(). In fact, get_pointing_pixels() is executed inside ScanningStrategy2map()."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"Now, the pointing TOD is calculated every month and stored in the map each time."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"ss.FP_theta = [0.0, 10.0]\nss.FP_phi = [0.0, 90.0]"},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"In this operation, the first component of the array represents the center of the focal plane, i.e. the boresight. On the other hand, the second component represents the detector that observes a point in the sky 10 degrees in theta direction and 90 degrees in phi direction away from the boresight."},{"title":"Scanning","page":"Scanning","location":"Scanning/","category":"page","text":"If you want to build a focal plane, just substitute its configuration into these arrays and you will be able to compute multi-channel pointings."},{"title":"Scanning strategy","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/#Scanning-strategy","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"To define the scanning strategy for a satellite, set the ScanningStrategy structure."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"mutable struct ScanningStrategy{T<:AbstractFloat, I<:Int, AA<:AbstractArray{T}, AS<:AbstractString}\n    nside::I\n    duration::I\n    sampling_rate::T\n    alpha::T\n    beta::T\n    prec_rpm::T\n    spin_rpm::T\n    hwp_rpm::T\n    FP_theta::AA\n    FP_phi::AA\n    start_point::AS\nend"},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"You can generate ScanningStrategy structure using gen_ScanningStrategy() function."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"This initial value of component of ScanningStrategy can be changed by specifying the gen_ScanningStrategy() argument when declaring ss like below."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"ss = gen_ScanningStrategy(alpha=60, prec_rpm=0.001, sampling_rate=5.0)"},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"In addition, you can directly access ss and interactively change its value."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"ss.nside = 256 #You can change a value\nss.spin_rpm = 0.04"},{"title":"Generate pointing TOD","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/#Generate-pointing-TOD","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"The information about the orientation of a satellite at a certain time is called pointing. The pointing is defined by (theta phi psi), where theta and phi are parameters of the 3D polar coordinates and psi is the angle between the scan direction and the meridian of the sky."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"Once the scanning strategy is determined, computing the pointing is straightforward."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"pointings = get_pointings(ss::ScanningStrategy, start::Int, stop::Int)\npix_tod, psi_tod, time_array = get_pointing_pixels(ss::ScanningStrategy, start::Int, stop::Int)"},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"Enter an integer value for the time to be calculated in the start and stop fields. The get_pointings() returns (theta phi psi) that indicate their pointing in chronological order. These are stored as an array of dictionary type, and can be accessed as follows."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"In[]: pointings[\"theta\"]\nOut[]: 14400×1 Matrix{Float64}:\n 1.1344640137963142\n 1.134461356131033\n 1.1344533831585348\n 1.1344400949488544\n..."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"The pointings[\"psi\"] contains the scanning angle according to the COSMO(HEALPix) definition. And pointings[\"time\"] contains the time used in the calculation."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"The get_pointing_pixels() does not allocate theta and phi arrays internally, but only allocates the minimum number of arrays needed to allocate the pixel TOD. Therefore, it runs faster than get_pointings(). In fact, get_pointing_pixels() is executed inside ScanningStrategy2map()."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"Now, the pointing TOD is calculated every month and stored in the map each time."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"ss.FP_theta = [0.0, 10.0]\nss.FP_phi = [0.0, 90.0]"},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"In this operation, the first component of the array represents the center of the focal plane, i.e. the boresight. On the other hand, the second component represents the detector that observes a point in the sky 10 degrees in theta direction and 90 degrees in phi direction away from the boresight."},{"title":"-","page":"-","location":".ipynb_checkpoints/Scanning-checkpoint/","category":"page","text":"If you want to build a focal plane, just substitute its configuration into these arrays and you will be able to compute multi-channel pointings."},{"title":"Mapmake","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/#Mapmake","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/#Pointings","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"To obtain TOD from Sky map, it is recommended to create a Pointing structure. The elemets are defined as:"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"mutable struct Pointings\n    x::AbstractFloat\n    y::AbstractFloat\n    z::AbstractFloat\n    θ::AbstractFloat # position of sky\n    φ::AbstractFloat # position of sky\n    Ω::Int           # Sky pixel index\n    ψ::AbstractFloat # Crossing angle\n    ϕ::AbstractFloat # HWP angle\n    ξ::AbstractFloat # mod2pi(2ϕ) + ψ\nend"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"It is convenient to use the pointings for the actual generation."},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"pointings(resol::Resolution, θ, φ, ψ, ϕ)"},{"title":"Available bolometric equations","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/#Available-bolometric-equations","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"true_signal(p::Pointings, maps::PolarizedHealpixMap, pixbuf, weightbuf)\ntrue_signal(p::Pointings, maps::PolarizedHealpixMap)\ninterp_signal(p::Pointings, maps::PolarizedHealpixMap)\ninterp_signal(p::Pointings, maps::PolarizedHealpixMap, pixbuf, weightbuf)"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"true_signal returns TOD which is defined by d_j = I(Omega) + Q(Omega)cos(2xi_j) + U(Omega)sin(2xi_j), where Omega shows sky pixel and xiequiv2phi+psi shows effective crossing angle which takes in to account the satellite crossing angle(psi) and its HWP angle(phi)."},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"interp_signal returns interpolated TOD which is defined pointings i.e. theta and varphi."},{"title":"Map-maker","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/#Map-maker","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"The binned_mapmake is available. "},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"binned_mapmake(ss::ScanningStrategy, division::Int, inputinfo::Falcons.InputInfo, signal)"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"The signal argument requires a function which has Pointings and PolarizedHealpixMap as arguments in the order. For example,"},{"title":"Pointings","page":"Pointings","location":"Mapmake/","category":"page","text":"binned_mapmake(ss::ScanningStrategy, division::Int, inputinfo::Falcons.InputInfo, true_signal)"},{"title":"Falcons","page":"Home","location":"#Falcons","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"This is the documentation of Falcons(Framework for Astrophysical Locus Computing ON the Sky), a package for fast simulation of satellite observations."},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"CMB polarimetric satellites such as LiteBIRD set up an appropriate scan strategy to reduce systematic errors, and Falcons can calculate all-sky hit maps, crosslink maps, etc. by simply setting the parameters of this scan strategy. It also supports observations with multi-channel detectors."},{"title":"Installation","page":"Home","location":"#Installation","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"From the Julia REPL, run"},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"import Pkg\nPkg.add(\"Falcons\")"},{"title":"Tutorial","page":"Home","location":"#Tutorial","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"The tutorial is available on the github page in jupyternotebook format. Please refer to here."},{"title":"Documentation","page":"Home","location":"#Documentation","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"The documentation was built using Documenter.jl."}]
